# Project rules and decisions

- Route highlight art on the tilemap court: the CourtRoute overlay takes its look from res://images/court_move_highlight.png, a rounded green panel. scripts/court.gd reads that image's fill colour and its corner-cut pixels, fills each selected cell, and rounds a corner only where both meeting sides are on the outside of the route (shared edges stay clean), so a route reads as one merged band with soft turns instead of a row of loose squares. The image's white rounded-off corners are treated as transparent background.
- Court front trim: the court's lower-right (front) diamond edge is lined with res://images/court_tile_edge.png, repeated along that single edge so it reads as a continuous band. Strip thickness scales with cell size; the repeat count is chosen so the band ends exactly on the right corner.
- Court is hand-painted tile data, not generated at runtime. res://tiles/court_tileset.tres (atlas res://tiles/court_tiles.svg, 10 tiles of 32x32) is a real TileSet: the sand checkerboard lives on the Court TileMapLayer and the white third/mid line tiles on CourtMarkings, both edited in the editor TileMap panel. scripts/court.gd is now only the teal route-highlight overlay (CourtRoute) and no longer paints the floor. The centre ring is still drawn.
- Player avatar (res://scripts/avatar.gd, class PlayerAvatar), built in code by scripts/ui.gd and pinned to the top-left corner: a rounded box with a black border and a dark fill, sized by PlayerAvatar.BOX_SIZE (120 px), the control bar shifted right to clear it. It shows one expression PNG at a time from res://images/players/<player_name>/player_expression_<expression>.png (default/happy/sad/angry/excited, player_name defaults to "nina"). A missing expression falls back to the default image and a missing default leaves the empty frame, so art can be dropped in later without breaking the build. Reaction rules: a clean catch (the coach's caught verdict, i.e. the token was set to take the feed) -> happy and the dropped-catch streak resets; a dropped feed, whether it settles as a loose ball or lands out of court -> sad, or angry on the second dropped catch in a row (AvatarPlayer.ANGRY_MISS_STREAK); a perfect shot -> excited; a missed shot -> angry. Shots do not exist in the prototype yet: Main.report_shot_result(made) is the seam to call from wherever a shot is later settled.
- Court presentation: 3/4 top-down isometric view. Logical grid coordinates project to a diamond (rotate 45 deg, Y halved); the TileMapLayer floor and painted court markings transform with the projection (flatten scale on parent CourtRig), while the player token and ball stay upright in screen space at projected positions. Input inverts the projection so drags still map to the same logical cells.
- Netball post: res://images/netball_post.png is a plain Sprite2D scene node (GoalPost, no script) standing on the right goal line at the middle cell, main.gd's POST_CELL (logical x = GRID_MAX, y = 5). It is placed and sized in _recompute_layout(): its base sits on grid_to_screen(POST_CELL), it stays upright in screen space like the token and the ball, and its art is scaled so the whole post is POST_HEIGHT_CELLS (2.0) court cells tall against the token's 1.2 CELL_FILL, with the sprite offset lifted by half its height so its feet land on the goal line. Post size therefore follows the court on every viewport resize.
- Route rating placement: the PERFECT / GOOD / OK rating word (ui.gd flash_rating) is drawn below the shot power meter rather than over the middle of the screen. The meter sits dead centre whenever the token holds the ball, and a clean catch flashes a rating at exactly that moment, so the two would otherwise fight for the same spot. The resting offset is measured from the screen centre - half of PowerGauge.GAUGE_SIZE.y, plus RATING_GAP_BELOW_GAUGE, plus half the word's own measured height - so the word keeps clearing the meter at any viewport size, and it still rises the last 34px into place the way it always did.
- Shot power meter (res://scripts/power_gauge.gd, class PowerGauge), built in code by scripts/ui.gd and anchored to the centre of the screen as a floating panel on the same ignore-only HUD root: it is visible only while the token is holding the ball, and the required range it asks for is sized from the straight-line distance between the token's cell and main.gd's POST_CELL - stood under the post wants little power, a shot from the far end wants nearly all of it, and the band narrows as that distance grows, so a long shot is a finer ask. Maintenance: main.gd owns possession (_has_ball goes true on a clean catch or a collected loose ball, and false when the coach takes the ball back) and pushes the distance through ui.set_power_gauge_distance(); the distance-to-power mapping itself lives in power_gauge.gd (MIN_DISTANCE_CELLS/MAX_DISTANCE_CELLS, BAND_CENTER_NEAR/FAR, BAND_HALF_NEAR/FAR). The meter stays up for POSSESSION_TIME (3.0s) once it appears: that window IS the round's COMPLETE timer, and letting it run out without a shot is classed as a missed shot - _shot_window_expired() reports it through report_shot_result(false) so the avatar goes angry, then settles through the normal MISS beat and the coach feeds the next ball. Shooting itself still does not exist: nothing consumes power(), no input is read and no shot is fired, so the needle sweeps the range by itself (SWEEP_SPEED) to show where the required band sits, and a future shot should drive it with set_power() and judge with in_accuracy_band() or against required_range(), reporting through Main.report_shot_result(made).
- Shot power selection is hold and release, owned by main.gd. The meter itself (power_gauge.gd) never reads input - it must stay MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE because it floats over the middle of the court, and a Control that stopped touches there would eat drag strokes (hard rule 4) - so Main._unhandled_input() watches the press and release: pressing while the token holds the ball calls _begin_power_charge(), which charges the needle from zero at CHARGE_SPEED, and letting go calls _release_power_charge(), which locks the value into _selected_power and pushes it out through ui.power_gauge_power(). A press only charges while _has_ball is true and a court drag only begins in READY, so the two inputs can never overlap: the hold picks the power, the release locks it, and nothing is fired. This is the selection that triggers the shot direction meter: _release_power_charge() raises the dial once the power is locked, so the direction is chosen against a settled power (see the direction-meter bullet at the end of this file). Nothing consumes _selected_power yet, and a possession whose clock runs out still ends the round as a missed shot.
- Shot direction meter (res://scripts/direction_gauge.gd, class DirectionGauge), built in code by scripts/ui.gd on the same ignore-only HUD root and anchored to the left of the centred power meter so the two never overlap: it is a semicircular dial built to the supplied reference art - a flat grey outer track, a coloured band inside it, a dark needle turning on a hub - where the needle position is a direction across the upper half of the screen, so dial-left is aimed left, dial-middle is straight up the screen and dial-right is aimed right. Green is the valid shot range and red is out of bounds: the green arc spans DIRECTION_TOLERANCE (0.05) around the optimal direction, i.e. 5% either side, which over the dial's 180 degree sweep is 9 degrees either way. The optimal direction is owned by main.gd (_optimal_direction_t()): the direction from the token's screen position to grid_to_screen(POST_CELL), read in screen space so the dial matches the 3/4 projection the player actually sees, with a post below the token clamped to the nearest end. It appears only once a power value has been selected, because a direction is chosen against a settled power: _release_power_charge() locks the power and then raises the dial, _sync_power_gauge() keeps the optimal direction in step while the meter is up, and it hides again at the next feed. Nothing selects a direction yet and no shot is fired, so the needle sweeps the dial by itself (SWEEP_SPEED) to show where the green range sits; a future direction move should drive it with set_direction() and judge with in_valid_range() or against valid_range(), reporting through Main.report_shot_result(made). Like the power meter it must stay MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE, or a Control over the court would eat drag strokes (hard rule 4).
- The shot is built (superseding the two notes above): res://scripts/shot_meter.gd (class ShotMeter) is the combined meter - the power bar and the direction dial in one control - built by scripts/ui.gd on the ignore-only HUD root at the centre of the screen, and PowerGauge/DirectionGauge (power_gauge.gd, direction_gauge.gd) are the earlier separate meters that ui.gd no longer builds; the files are left in place but unused. The two halves are answered in order because the second is only meaningful once the first is settled: a hold charges the power needle and the release locks the value and lights the dial up (until then the dial is grey and inert), then a press that lands on the dial (ShotMeter.AIM_HIT) starts the aim, a slide steers the needle, and the release is the shot. main.gd drives the whole sequence from _unhandled_input via _press_with_ball()/_release_with_ball(), and keeps the meter in step with possession, distance and the optimal direction in _sync_shot_meter(); the meter itself never reads input and must stay MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE (hard rule 4).
- How a shot is judged and flown (main.gd _fire_shot()): the values the player locked are compared against the very ranges the meter was showing - _selected_power against ui.shot_meter_required_range() and _selected_direction against ui.shot_meter_valid_range(). Inside BOTH, the ball goes through the ring; outside either, it comes down beside it, offset by how far outside the shot was along the line to the ring (power = short/long) and across it (dial = wide left/right), so a miss reads as a mistake rather than as random. The ball is not simulated or collided: the flight, the ring and the drop through the net are all drawn by _draw_shot_ball() from the same path the verdict is read off, and the verdict is only called in _resolve_shot() once the ball has come down, so the GOAL / SHOT MISSED beat comes after the ball. A make reports report_shot_result(true) (avatar excited) and a miss report_shot_result(false) (angry), then the coach feeds again. A possession that times out with no shot is still a missed shot through _shot_window_expired(), and the meter hides the moment the ball leaves (the token no longer holds it).
- The ring the shot aims at is read off the post art, not guessed: res://tools/inspect_post.tscn prints the geometry of res://images/netball_post.png (it finds the only red-dominant region, which is the ring), and main.gd's HOOP_HEIGHT_FRAC (0.857, the ring centre above the sprite's feet as a fraction of drawn height) and HOOP_X_FRAC (0.404, the ring centre across the drawn width; the pole is at 0.5, so the ring overhangs the court side) come from it, with HOOP_RADIUS_FRAC for the ring's projected size. Because the post sprite is scaled so the whole image is POST_HEIGHT_CELLS tall, the flight, the ring and the drop all follow the post at every viewport size. Re-run the tool and re-measure those three constants if the post art is ever replaced.
- Both halves of the shot meter are now the same gesture (superseding the "press the dial and slide" wording in the note above): hold and the needle moves by itself, release and the value it was on is locked in. The power needle climbs from zero (CHARGE_SPEED) and is clamped at full; the direction needle SWEEPS back and forth between the two extreme values of the dial and bounces off them (SWEEP_SPEED, 0.55 of the sweep per second, so a full traverse takes a bit under two seconds, and the green arc at DIRECTION_TOLERANCE is 0.10 of the sweep wide, so the needle is inside it for roughly 180ms at a time). The green arc is therefore a timing window to catch rather than a spot to drag onto, and that is exactly the point: a shot is successful only when the release leaves the aim needle inside the green. Every aim sweep restarts from the dial's low end so the timing reads the same way each attempt. ShotMeter.aim_at() is renamed begin_aim(): it no longer reads where the press landed to place the needle, it only refuses a press that landed on the power bar (BAR_HIT, which re-picks the power instead) and then starts the sweep. Main's InputEventScreenDrag / InputEventMouseMotion handlers no longer feed the dial at all - the needle is not the finger's - so a drag during a shot does nothing and only a court route still follows the finger. Main's SHOT_HINT_AIM instruction text is now "Hold to sweep the dial, release on the green to shoot."
- The shot direction gets a possession window of its own, once per possession. Choosing a direction is a second timed decision, and the power hold has usually eaten part of the original window before the dial is even live, so main.gd tops the clock up the moment a power value is locked: _release_with_ball() adds AIM_WINDOW_TIME (3.0) to _complete_timer on top of the POSSESSION_TIME (3.0) the possession started with, giving the aim roughly six seconds in total. A _aim_window_granted flag makes it once-only, so re-picking the power (a press on the power bar, which calls _clear_selected_shot()) cannot keep topping the clock back up and holding a round open indefinitely; the flag resets in _start_test() with the rest of the per-round state. Only the clock changed - _shot_window_expired() still classes a possession whose window runs out with no shot as a missed shot and feeds the next ball.
- How the shot flight is built, and why it must stay that way: main.gd's _fire_shot() reads the ring into court space with _hoop_logical(), which returns the point on the FLOOR under the ring plus HOOP_HEIGHT_CELLS as its height. It must NOT unproject the ring's SCREEN point - the ring is drawn lifted off the floor by its own height, and that inverse is the floor projection, so it reads the height as distance up the court and throws the ball at the middle of the court while the verdict is still decided from the meter values. This was a real bug: the ball flew nowhere near the hoop and was still given as a GOAL. The flight now carries a GROUND track (_shot_ground_at(), a projected bowed curve to the floor point under the ring) and a HEIGHT above the floor (_shot_height_at()) separately, and the ball is drawn at the ground point lifted by that height. The height leaves the hands at SHOT_RELEASE_HEIGHT (0.55 cells) and is EXACTLY at the ring's height on arrival, plus arc lift through the middle scaled by distance (SHOT_ARC_MIN/MAX, SHOT_ARC_FULL_CELLS). A height term built from sin(t * PI) alone is zero on arrival, which is the other half of that bug - it would make a shot land at the foot of the post. Misses: the power error carries the ball short or long as a fraction of the range (SHOT_MISS_REACH, floored by SHOT_MISS_MIN_REACH) and the dial error swings it wide by an angle (SHOT_MISS_ANGLE, floored by SHOT_MISS_MIN_ANGLE), so a miss reads as short, long or wide rather than random; _shot_miss_floor is where the ball actually came down and where the landing mark is left. The error helpers are _shot_miss_error() and _side_error(). Flight time is SHOT_FLIGHT_MIN plus SHOT_FLIGHT_PER_CELL per cell of range, clamped by SHOT_FLIGHT_MAX.
- The scenario quick picks (A, B, C, D, E) and the RESET button are removed from the HUD at the user's request, but the logic behind them is deliberately kept. ui.gd's _build() no longer creates them, so the top bar is now just a spacer plus the status pill and the Debug and Grid toggles, _reset_btn stays null and _scenario_btns stays empty. SCENARIO_LABELS / SCENARIO_TIPS, the _reset_btn and _scenario_btns fields, and the bind() wiring are all left in place, and Main.random_test() / Main.load_scenario() are untouched - main.gd still passes its "reset" and "scenario" callbacks through bind(), and bind() null-checks the reset button so the connection is a no-op while it does not exist. Rebuilding the buttons in _build() is all that is needed to bring the controls back.
- Goal scoring uses an invisible Goal/ScoringZone Area2D with its own logical center, height and radius. GoalPost remains visual-only Sprite2D art; the shot path targets the scoring zone and does not derive gameplay collision from the post texture, pivot or scale.
- Goal/HoopTarget is the authoritative shot destination marker. It is positioned at the actual hoop location independently of the GoalPost sprite origin, while Goal/ScoringZone follows the marker with separately tuned dimensions and entry rules.
- GoalZone is the single tuning surface for goal_cell, hoop_height_cells, scoring_zone_width_cells, scoring_zone_height_cells, scoring_zone_depth_cells, required_trajectory_direction, trajectory_tolerance_degrees, and require_descending_entry. The initial zone is intentionally forgiving and the post stays presentation-only.
- Shot-ball spin is visual-only: the seam starts at the player-to-HoopTarget angle, then a Tween spins it for the flight and drop, with selected power controlling turn count and trajectory angle controlling spin direction and a small angle-based adjustment.
